WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.140 --> 00:00:03.390
Hey guys, before we get started, I wanted
to tell you about a really exciting development

00:00:03.390 --> 00:00:04.510
for Wisecrack.

00:00:04.510 --> 00:00:06.669
We recently launched a partnership with medium.com.

00:00:06.669 --> 00:00:10.890
So that means we're now publishing two to
three articles a week on all the things you've

00:00:10.890 --> 00:00:13.000
come to expect from us - plus, more.

00:00:13.000 --> 00:00:18.900
We got a brand new story on the 30-50 hogs
meme, my exclusive thoughts on Once Upon a

00:00:18.900 --> 00:00:24.550
Time in Hollywood, as well as awesome pieces
on the KFC dating game, Fleabag, Japanese

00:00:24.550 --> 00:00:25.690
toilets, and way more.

00:00:25.690 --> 00:00:31.650
So go to medium.com/wisecrack and subscribe
to the publication or click up here to get

00:00:31.650 --> 00:00:33.190
taken to the page.

00:00:33.190 --> 00:00:40.710
Thanks everyone, and now, on with the show.

00:00:40.710 --> 00:00:41.710
What's up guys?

00:00:41.710 --> 00:00:42.710
Jared again.

00:00:42.710 --> 00:00:45.039
Media has a huge effect on the way we view
the world.

00:00:45.039 --> 00:00:47.000
Jaws made everyone afraid of sharks.

00:00:47.000 --> 00:00:49.550
It made everyone afraid of clowns.

00:00:49.550 --> 00:00:53.969
Big made everybody afraid of being rapidly
aged by a weird fortune telling machine.

00:00:53.969 --> 00:00:58.589
But what about when media actually portrays
the real world in all of its whale abusers,

00:00:58.589 --> 00:01:01.010
duck hunters, and baby beauty queens?

00:01:01.010 --> 00:01:05.379
If imaginary house elves can make us more
sympathetic to inequality what does watching

00:01:05.379 --> 00:01:07.840
actual factual humans do to us?

00:01:07.840 --> 00:01:13.180
We’re in the midst of a nonfiction media
boom - both documentary and reality TV.

00:01:13.180 --> 00:01:19.220
There were 750 reality shows in 2015, leading
some critics to say that it’s even remade

00:01:19.220 --> 00:01:21.420
television culture entirely.

00:01:21.420 --> 00:01:27.399
Similarly, documentary has been cinema's unlikely
Cinderella in recent years, with three documentaries

00:01:27.399 --> 00:01:32.149
in 2018 cracking the list of top 30 highest-grossing
documentaries since 1982, and that’s not

00:01:32.149 --> 00:01:36.450
even counting the insane proliferation of
documentary filmmaking happening on streaming

00:01:36.450 --> 00:01:37.450
sites.

00:01:37.450 --> 00:01:41.940
If fictional media changes the way we view
the world, how does nonfiction media contribute

00:01:41.940 --> 00:01:44.360
to or inform our worldview?

00:01:44.360 --> 00:01:49.250
In short, what might documentaries and reality
TV be doing to our brainy parts?

00:01:49.250 --> 00:01:53.030
Let’s find out in this week’s Wisecrack
Edition on The Philosophy of Documentaries

00:01:53.030 --> 00:01:54.680
and Reality TV.

00:01:54.680 --> 00:01:57.140
And spoilers ahead for some documentaries.

00:01:57.140 --> 00:02:01.729
To figure out how documentaries and reality
TV affect us, we first need to understand

00:02:01.729 --> 00:02:03.310
what this stuff is.

00:02:03.310 --> 00:02:07.080
Documenting reality is literally as old as
filmmaking itself.

00:02:07.080 --> 00:02:11.200
The earliest films, made around the end of
the 19th century, were pretty much all super

00:02:11.200 --> 00:02:16.000
short visual accounts of real life events,
like workers peacing out from their factory

00:02:16.000 --> 00:02:20.440
or an elephant being cruelly tortured for
our entertainment, which you can thank your

00:02:20.440 --> 00:02:23.780
favorite lightbulb inventing scoundrel, Thomas
Edison for.

00:02:23.780 --> 00:02:27.870
Yes, this elephant is about to be electrocuted
and no we’re not going to show you cause

00:02:27.870 --> 00:02:29.070
fuck that!

00:02:29.070 --> 00:02:32.930
But the term documentary has always implied
more than just taking a camera and shooting

00:02:32.930 --> 00:02:37.720
whatever goes down in the life of your local
yodeling community, though, by all means,

00:02:37.720 --> 00:02:38.720
go for it!

00:02:38.720 --> 00:02:42.310
The word “documentary” was first used
by Scottish filmmaker John Grierson in the

00:02:42.310 --> 00:02:46.989
1920s, and he described it as “the creative
treatment of actuality.”

00:02:46.989 --> 00:02:49.260
Even this definition says a lot.

00:02:49.260 --> 00:02:55.989
Applying creativity to actuality already suggests
that it's distinct from actual actuality.

00:02:55.989 --> 00:03:00.750
Rather than showing neutral uncut footage
of an event, a documentarian will use a variety

00:03:00.750 --> 00:03:05.260
of tools, including editing, to string together
a compelling story.

00:03:05.260 --> 00:03:09.800
Since its early days, documentary has had
a complicated relationship with the truth.

00:03:09.800 --> 00:03:14.330
Take the 1922 documentary Nanook of the North,
which depicted the life of a man from the

00:03:14.330 --> 00:03:15.500
Inuk tribe.

00:03:15.500 --> 00:03:20.950
The film is definitely misleading - for starters,
Nanook wasn’t even named Nanook - His real

00:03:20.950 --> 00:03:26.519
name was Allakariallak, but the director,
Robert J Flaherty, thought that was unmarketable.

00:03:26.519 --> 00:03:30.910
Flaherty also staged several scenes which
were historically inaccurate, showing Inuk

00:03:30.910 --> 00:03:34.190
life the way it would have been a century
prior.

00:03:34.190 --> 00:03:38.870
For instance, though Nanook typically hunted
walruses with a simple gun, Flaherty asked

00:03:38.870 --> 00:03:41.560
him to use a harpoon for the sake of drama.

00:03:41.560 --> 00:03:46.060
The film’s various elements compound to
present Nanook and his family as childish,

00:03:46.060 --> 00:03:48.209
primitive and a bit silly.

00:03:48.209 --> 00:03:53.299
It helps here to think about a different definition
of documentary, as an “imaginative representation

00:03:53.299 --> 00:03:56.260
of an actual historical reality.”

00:03:56.260 --> 00:04:00.370
Documentary is supposed to have fidelity to
a particular historical reality - whether

00:04:00.370 --> 00:04:05.860
its showing an Inuk tribe in the 1920s or
a wealthy Florida Housewife in 2012.

00:04:05.860 --> 00:04:10.959
Flaherty distorted that reality by depicting
historically inaccurate customs.

00:04:10.959 --> 00:04:15.230
As we move forward to the 1930s and 40s, the
way documentary bends the truth is perhaps

00:04:15.230 --> 00:04:20.500
most glaringly apparent, when just about every
major country put out its own form of propaganda

00:04:20.500 --> 00:04:23.230
trying to get people jazzed about war and
whatnot.

00:04:23.230 --> 00:04:27.480
Most notably, Triumph of the Will solidified
Hitler’s power and public persona.

00:04:27.480 --> 00:04:32.160
Now, we seem to talk about this movie more
often than a 1930s German soldier at a dive

00:04:32.160 --> 00:04:35.230
bar, but I swear its relevant.

00:04:35.230 --> 00:04:40.270
In this film, we see the way subtle choices
in cinematography can drastically alter a

00:04:40.270 --> 00:04:44.600
documentary’s meaning and fidelity to the
truth: Look at the way Hitler appears on camera,

00:04:44.600 --> 00:04:50.350
from a low-angle shot, he seems taller, almost
larger than life, and certainly more powerful.

00:04:50.350 --> 00:04:51.350
Gross.

00:04:51.350 --> 00:04:56.410
Anyway, that same year, the “talking head”
or the interview style in which an often boring

00:04:56.410 --> 00:05:00.440
person stares at the screen and talks for
awhile, would be introduced via the documentary

00:05:00.440 --> 00:05:05.470
Housing Problems, as an attempt to let individuals
tell their own story, rather than having it

00:05:05.470 --> 00:05:08.460
dictated by a “voice of god” narrator.

00:05:08.460 --> 00:05:11.740
This was an honest attempt to make storytelling
more truthful.

00:05:11.740 --> 00:05:16.990
In the 1960s, cameras became smaller, allowing
film crews to become more mobile.

00:05:16.990 --> 00:05:21.990
This facilitated a new form of documentary
practiced by filmmakers like the Maysles Brothers.

00:05:21.990 --> 00:05:27.910
It was called “cinema verite,” which translates
to “truthful cinema” or “cinema of truth.”

00:05:27.910 --> 00:05:32.530
Functionally, cinema verite filmmakers will
follow their subject around, passively observing

00:05:32.530 --> 00:05:37.940
their lives without interfering, as the subject
ignores the camera, all in an attempt to more

00:05:37.940 --> 00:05:40.070
accurately capture the truth.

00:05:40.070 --> 00:05:42.680
Of course, that caused problems of its own.

00:05:42.680 --> 00:05:48.070
Because the camera movements imply honesty,
we assume that we’re watching actual reality.

00:05:48.070 --> 00:05:52.070
Some filmmakers, such as Richard Pennebaker
who made the Bob Dylan documentary Don’t

00:05:52.070 --> 00:05:57.260
Look Back were sensitive to this, and purposely
kept aesthetically unpleasing cinematography

00:05:57.260 --> 00:06:02.920
flourishes to argue for the film’s truthfulness
- showing the audience the camera eye focusing,

00:06:02.920 --> 00:06:05.240
or keeping awkward zooms.

00:06:05.240 --> 00:06:08.900
Filmmakers began experimenting further with
the very nature of what it meant to engage

00:06:08.900 --> 00:06:11.780
in nonfiction storytelling.

00:06:11.780 --> 00:06:16.460
Documentarian Errol Morris encorporated dramatic
reenactments to simulate a real-life murder

00:06:16.460 --> 00:06:20.460
in his 1988 film The Thin Blue Line, to some
criticism.

00:06:20.460 --> 00:06:25.970
By the early 90s, more intimate documentaries
became popular, functioning as cinematic autobiographies

00:06:25.970 --> 00:06:28.470
or a close look at an artist’s family.

00:06:28.470 --> 00:06:34.290
In recent years, films also became “voicier,”
as in the work of Michael Moore who editorializes

00:06:34.290 --> 00:06:40.310
and inserts himself into his own plots, less
documenting reality than crafting it.

00:06:40.310 --> 00:06:45.080
The 2000s were dominated by documentaries
that made strong arguments like An Inconvenient

00:06:45.080 --> 00:06:48.160
Truth, Super Size Me, and Fahrenheit 9/11.

00:06:48.160 --> 00:06:52.610
In Super Size Me, the documentarian is the
opposite of the neutral observer - he literally

00:06:52.610 --> 00:06:57.340
turns the camera on himself and his rapidly
deteriorating liver (Which, if we’re speaking

00:06:57.340 --> 00:07:01.560
about ethics, becomes a little more problematic
due to recent revelations that director Morgan

00:07:01.560 --> 00:07:04.010
Spurlok was a longtime alcoholic.

00:07:04.010 --> 00:07:06.450
So can we really blame only Fast food?)

00:07:06.450 --> 00:07:11.410
In a lot of ways, these films are more self
aware than your classic cinema verite - their

00:07:11.410 --> 00:07:13.930
artifice is right on the surface.

00:07:13.930 --> 00:07:18.810
For example, when Michael Moore storms a General
Motors shareholders meeting, we clearly know

00:07:18.810 --> 00:07:21.130
we’re not witnessing neutral reality.

00:07:21.130 --> 00:07:26.310
At the same time, modern filmmakers are also,
arguably, taking more creative license with

00:07:26.310 --> 00:07:27.350
the truth.

00:07:27.350 --> 00:07:32.060
There is perhaps no greater narrative artifice
than the satisfying, climactic ending.

00:07:32.060 --> 00:07:36.550
Many a documentarian has grappled with this,
but perhaps none more interestingly than filmmaker

00:07:36.550 --> 00:07:40.940
Ben Berman in his documentary, Untitled Amazing
Jonathan Documentary.

00:07:40.940 --> 00:07:44.580
In it, the filmmaker shadows the titular,
terminally-ill magician.

00:07:44.580 --> 00:07:48.530
The problem is: Several other people are doing
the exact same thing.

00:07:48.530 --> 00:07:52.630
Looking for a way to make his story stand
out, Berman, at one point, believes that the

00:07:52.630 --> 00:07:56.430
only satisfying conclusion would be Jonathan
dying.

00:07:56.430 --> 00:08:00.710
Waiting for your subject to die is pretty
blatantly macabre, and Jonathan even calls

00:08:00.710 --> 00:08:01.720
the guy out on it.

00:08:01.720 --> 00:08:04.960
"You're just waitin', you're just waitin'-
you're biding your time until I die and then

00:08:04.960 --> 00:08:06.270
you get that ending you want."

00:08:06.270 --> 00:08:11.360
Here, Berman confronts the inherent ethical
dilemmas of imposing requirements of a satisfying

00:08:11.360 --> 00:08:13.530
story on reality.

00:08:13.530 --> 00:08:17.630
Luckily, he finds an ending without killing
the Amazing Jonathan.

00:08:17.630 --> 00:08:22.030
But beyond imposing artificial beginnings
and ends, creatively tinkering with reality

00:08:22.030 --> 00:08:24.060
has other complications.

00:08:24.060 --> 00:08:28.390
Take the issue of characterization, or the
way that characters are constructed in a given

00:08:28.390 --> 00:08:29.880
piece of media.

00:08:29.880 --> 00:08:34.610
Scholar Carl Plantinga notes that, “in any
documentary, the images and sounds that represent

00:08:34.610 --> 00:08:40.450
the character are not neutral and transparent,
but carefully constructed and chosen to portray

00:08:40.450 --> 00:08:42.050
them in a specific way.”

00:08:42.050 --> 00:08:47.370
He adds that the process of “selection and
omission, emphasis,[plotting] and point of

00:08:47.370 --> 00:08:51.460
view” all create additional ethical dilemmas
for documentarians.

00:08:51.460 --> 00:08:55.810
The way most documentarians will deal with
this is to make characters that are, as Plantinga

00:08:55.810 --> 00:09:01.380
puts it, “flat” or simple, possessing
one or two central characteristics.

00:09:01.380 --> 00:09:05.680
For example, in a Michael Moore documentary
like Roger and Me which serves as an indictment

00:09:05.680 --> 00:09:10.270
of the very very wealthy, you’re not going
to see General Motors CEO Roger Smith patting

00:09:10.270 --> 00:09:14.250
his son on the back, or giving a dollar to
a homeless man, because the message of the

00:09:14.250 --> 00:09:16.680
film is that he’s an evil asshole.

00:09:16.680 --> 00:09:21.330
Forming “round” or complex characters,
according to Plantinga, is much more difficult.

00:09:21.330 --> 00:09:26.140
And so, for the most part, every aspect of
filmmaking and editing will serve to emphasize

00:09:26.140 --> 00:09:28.750
a central quality of any given character.

00:09:28.750 --> 00:09:33.170
(Now, this is also true of fiction… but
that’s part of what makes it fiction.)

00:09:33.170 --> 00:09:38.080
Flat characters seems like as good a segway
to reality TV as we could ask for.

00:09:38.080 --> 00:09:41.580
Now, reality TV has always been documentary’s
bastard son.

00:09:41.580 --> 00:09:47.140
But we just want to say here: Most documentarians
struggle deeply with the ethics of their craft.

00:09:47.140 --> 00:09:48.310
Reality TV?

00:09:48.310 --> 00:09:49.310
Not so much.

00:09:49.310 --> 00:09:51.890
It’s less respected and taken less seriously.

00:09:51.890 --> 00:09:56.300
It’s also frequently charged with taking
a giant Nerf gun to reality.

00:09:56.300 --> 00:09:58.200
But the truth is more complicated.

00:09:58.200 --> 00:10:03.010
Some of the earliest iterations of “televisual
reality" fall somewhere between the categories

00:10:03.010 --> 00:10:05.420
of documentary and reality TV.

00:10:05.420 --> 00:10:09.440
This includes Candid Camera, which used a
hidden camera to film normal people reacting

00:10:09.440 --> 00:10:13.600
to practical jokes on and off from 1948 to
2014.

00:10:13.600 --> 00:10:19.620
Similarly, An American Family used what television
scholar Misha Kavka calls a “live-in camera”

00:10:19.620 --> 00:10:23.891
to chronicle the oftentimes boring pursuits
of one upper middle class American family

00:10:23.891 --> 00:10:25.610
to the delight of millions.

00:10:25.610 --> 00:10:31.880
Kavka goes on to separate the history of “real”
reality tv into three distinct categories.

00:10:31.880 --> 00:10:36.930
The first is the camcorder generation which
hit a stride in 1989 with a boom of crime

00:10:36.930 --> 00:10:41.880
and emergency programmes that offer an “ultra
verite” experience through the use of shaky

00:10:41.880 --> 00:10:43.340
ride-along footage.

00:10:43.340 --> 00:10:48.300
These shows were super real, but they also
vacillated between being very exciting and

00:10:48.300 --> 00:10:50.220
very, very boring.

00:10:50.220 --> 00:10:52.070
So reality TV regrouped.

00:10:52.070 --> 00:10:56.800
The second generation began in 1999, and that’s
when things get gnarly.

00:10:56.800 --> 00:11:02.150
We start seeing competition shows like Survivor,
The Amazing Race, American Idol and America’s

00:11:02.150 --> 00:11:06.550
Next Top Model, along with surveillance shows
like Big Brother and The Real World.

00:11:06.550 --> 00:11:11.440
As Kavka notes, the genre began developing
“its own format rules, production practices

00:11:11.440 --> 00:11:16.920
and audience expectations” such as confessional
interviews, elimination ceremonies, and plenty

00:11:16.920 --> 00:11:18.510
of ugly crying.

00:11:18.510 --> 00:11:23.190
Kavka notes that this era of reality tv “found
its place in the millennial culture imaginary

00:11:23.190 --> 00:11:28.760
by openly combining actuality and artifice
in ways that broke ratings records and caused

00:11:28.760 --> 00:11:30.400
wide-scale debate.”

00:11:30.400 --> 00:11:34.630
Intriguingly enough, every twenty-two minute
episode of the Real World was the result of

00:11:34.630 --> 00:11:40.920
seventy hours worth of raw footage, meaning
this version of reality is… has to be...

00:11:40.920 --> 00:11:42.930
Cherry-picked and skewed.

00:11:42.930 --> 00:11:47.350
These shows frequently insert their casts
into artificially constructed situations that

00:11:47.350 --> 00:11:49.060
generate lots of stress.

00:11:49.060 --> 00:11:53.790
Then, they actively facilitate the social
destruction that follows, often by staging

00:11:53.790 --> 00:11:57.710
and producing scenes that “complete” the
narrative arc.

00:11:57.710 --> 00:11:59.220
Also - booze.

00:11:59.220 --> 00:12:02.440
Not exactly the kind of thing the Maysles
brothers would have been about.

00:12:02.440 --> 00:12:07.470
The third era of reality TV kind of crept
up starting in 2002 with what Kavka describes

00:12:07.470 --> 00:12:13.190
as “a gradual but noticeable change… from
an emphasis on documenting ordinary life to

00:12:13.190 --> 00:12:16.340
manufacturing celebrity out of the everyday.”

00:12:16.340 --> 00:12:21.050
The very phrase “manufacturing celebrity”
not so subtly hints at the artifice inherent

00:12:21.050 --> 00:12:26.160
in these shows, where characters seek “the
insubstantial but highly desirable commodity

00:12:26.160 --> 00:12:27.470
called ‘fame.’”

00:12:27.470 --> 00:12:31.960
People’s onscreen behavior is increasingly
motivated less by genuine emotions than by

00:12:31.960 --> 00:12:36.820
the desire to attract as much attention as
possible, a phenomenon best encapsulated by

00:12:36.820 --> 00:12:42.310
Kris Jenner, who could turn a bunion on Kim’s
big toe into an international news story,

00:12:42.310 --> 00:12:43.980
and probably already has.

00:12:43.980 --> 00:12:49.360
What we’re seeing is that, far more so than
documentary, reality TV involves deliberate

00:12:49.360 --> 00:12:54.450
departures from “truth” be it in the form
of hawk-eyed producers staging dramatic scenes,

00:12:54.450 --> 00:12:59.060
or a drunk housewife doing it herself in hopes
of scoring her own vodka brand.

00:12:59.060 --> 00:13:03.600
If the second generation of reality TV birthed
several distinctive genre tools, the third

00:13:03.600 --> 00:13:05.870
generation solidified them.

00:13:05.870 --> 00:13:11.090
As a result, certain aspects of reality tv
are so familiar to us, from the tearful confession

00:13:11.090 --> 00:13:15.470
of past trauma, to the manufactured fights,
to the words “you’re going home.”

00:13:15.470 --> 00:13:20.510
So what is the net result of watching tv and
movies that are optimized and dramatized but

00:13:20.510 --> 00:13:23.650
self-present to audiences as real?

00:13:23.650 --> 00:13:28.020
One consequence that really resonates with
us is that documentaries and reality TV make

00:13:28.020 --> 00:13:34.160
us narrativize reality, that is, see our own
lives and the lives of those around us as

00:13:34.160 --> 00:13:37.620
stories with beginnings, middles and ends.

00:13:37.620 --> 00:13:42.260
As philosopher Alex Rosenberg puts it in his
book How History Gets Things Wrong, “We

00:13:42.260 --> 00:13:47.620
humans have an insatiable appetite for stories
with identifiable heroes, the tension of a

00:13:47.620 --> 00:13:53.070
quest, obstacles overcome and a happy (or
at least emotionally satisfying) ending.”

00:13:53.070 --> 00:13:57.250
Quick note: Though Rosenberg is talking more
broadly about the way we employ historical

00:13:57.250 --> 00:14:03.010
narratives, we think it can be usefully applied
to documentary and reality TV as forms of

00:14:03.010 --> 00:14:04.010
entertainment.

00:14:04.010 --> 00:14:07.700
Rosenberg also notes that we use historical
narratives to make sense of the present and

00:14:07.700 --> 00:14:09.610
even predict the future.

00:14:09.610 --> 00:14:13.800
We may watch a makeover show and think our
lives are just one over-night transformation

00:14:13.800 --> 00:14:15.660
away from fulfillment.

00:14:15.660 --> 00:14:21.650
And how could watching hours of banal Kardashian
drama not make us think that maybe, just maybe,

00:14:21.650 --> 00:14:26.210
our lives might be interesting enough to be
chronicled for the world to snarkily enjoy?

00:14:26.210 --> 00:14:31.210
What’s more, Rosenberg argues, stories with
satisfying narratives are like aloe vera for

00:14:31.210 --> 00:14:36.150
our brains, soothing us and lulling us into
a false sense of understanding.

00:14:36.150 --> 00:14:40.260
Susan Boyle may have never been kissed, but
boy did she wow the judges on Britain's Got

00:14:40.260 --> 00:14:41.260
Talent.

00:14:41.260 --> 00:14:48.720
Here, Susan’s real-life suffering is first
used to make us care about her.

00:14:48.720 --> 00:14:57.440
Then, her objectively-excellent performance
and rousing reception offer a compelling resolution

00:14:57.440 --> 00:15:10.930
that ultimately redeems her and her suffering.

00:15:10.930 --> 00:15:13.120
It wasn’t for nothing, after all!

00:15:13.120 --> 00:15:18.220
Or, even if we’re healthily skeptical of
a Chopped narrative, - "I just feel like people

00:15:18.220 --> 00:15:22.240
find reasons to hate on me, cuz I'm definitely
different.

00:15:22.240 --> 00:15:26.529
because" - some part of us is stoked that
this girl’s suffering at the hands of bullies

00:15:26.529 --> 00:15:29.730
has been redeemed by winning Chopped Jr.

00:15:29.730 --> 00:15:33.330
All of this fundamentally limits our understanding
of the world.

00:15:33.330 --> 00:15:38.480
As scholar Ib Bondebjerg writes, summarizing
the work of neurologist Antonio Damasio, “our

00:15:38.480 --> 00:15:43.680
sense of self and our ability to interpret
the world are based on emotional and narrative

00:15:43.680 --> 00:15:44.680
structures...

00:15:44.680 --> 00:15:48.920
Narrative Structures are part of the very
fabric of our mind and imagination.”

00:15:48.920 --> 00:15:52.850
So our brains crave narrative like a unicorn
craves sparkles.

00:15:52.850 --> 00:15:56.670
And reality TV sells us that narrative as
truth.

00:15:56.670 --> 00:16:00.360
As a result, watching The Voice may subtly
makes us think that everything we do in life

00:16:00.360 --> 00:16:04.710
can be seen as us triumphing over our central
life struggle.

00:16:04.710 --> 00:16:09.291
Watching The Bachelor subtly makes us eager
to find our “happy ever after,” a term

00:16:09.291 --> 00:16:20.779
that is certainly ridiculous to anyone who's
ever been married.

00:16:20.779 --> 00:16:25.870
And watching My Super Sweet Sixteen not so
subtly informs us that absurdly wealthy adolescents

00:16:25.870 --> 00:16:27.720
are monsters which…

00:16:27.720 --> 00:16:29.589
no comment.

00:16:29.589 --> 00:16:34.470
This can be a problem because real life isn’t
made up of neat narrative arcs and clean,

00:16:34.470 --> 00:16:35.470
happy endings.

00:16:35.470 --> 00:16:40.550
It’s messy and things happen for no reason,
and not every choice you make is motivated

00:16:40.550 --> 00:16:44.160
by the fact that the neighborhood asshole
Ricky made you wear your underwear as a hat

00:16:44.160 --> 00:16:45.600
in third grade.

00:16:45.600 --> 00:16:50.250
You probably won’t have a hero’s journey,
and if you do, it probably won’t take place

00:16:50.250 --> 00:16:51.680
in three acts.

00:16:51.680 --> 00:16:56.240
But watching enough nonfiction media has a
tendency to confuse us.

00:16:56.240 --> 00:17:01.029
So what is a responsible, non-masochistic
movie-watcher supposed to do with all this

00:17:01.029 --> 00:17:02.029
information?

00:17:02.029 --> 00:17:06.459
Should you protest Chopped’s false narrativizing
or skip that super compelling documentary

00:17:06.459 --> 00:17:10.930
on Kim Jung Il impersonators, which doesn’t
exist but goddamn it should.

00:17:10.930 --> 00:17:14.610
We love documentaries just as much as the
next film nerd, and we’d never ruin The

00:17:14.610 --> 00:17:16.350
Great British Bake-off for you.

00:17:16.350 --> 00:17:20.890
We just feel like, as media fanatics, we all
should be a little more aware of the way everyday

00:17:20.890 --> 00:17:25.709
storytelling affects the way we view our own
lives and our own stories.

00:17:25.709 --> 00:17:27.120
But what do you guys think?

00:17:27.120 --> 00:17:31.410
Are documentaries and reality TV ruining the
way you understand the world?

00:17:31.410 --> 00:17:35.059
Or is narrativizing reality just a necessary
part of being human?

00:17:35.059 --> 00:17:36.750
Let us know what you think in the comments.

